{
  "chapter": "retrieval/ingestion-pipeline",
  "tier": "build",
  "notes": [
    "The discourse is about parsers; the incidents are about lifecycle. This builds the half",
    "nobody writes about: change detection, deletion, idempotency and the manifest.",
    "Change is detected by hashing the normalized content, never by a timestamp, because source",
    "systems touch timestamps for reasons unrelated to content.",
    "Chunk identity is deterministic — document id plus index — so a re-run after a partial",
    "failure replaces chunks instead of doubling them.",
    "Most pipelines only add. Reconciliation lists what the source has, diffs it against the",
    "index, and tombstones the difference, which is the only thing standing between you and",
    "citing a retracted policy.",
    "A document that cannot be assigned a tenant fails ingestion rather than being indexed",
    "untagged, and a parse failure is named in the manifest rather than silently skipped.",
    "The pipeline version is written on every row, so a version bump reindexes everything and a",
    "partial migration is diagnosable instead of mysterious."
  ],
